> Write a simple website in HTML. When a user clicks the button, it shows a random joke from a list of 4 jokes.
[0m```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Random Joke Generator</title>
<style>
body {
  font-family: sans-serif;
  text-align: center;
}

#joke-container {
  margin-top: 20px;
  font-size: 18px;
}

button {
  padding: 10px 20px;
  font-size: 16px;
  background-color: #4CAF50;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  border-radius: 5px;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #3e8e41;
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>

<button onclick="generateJoke()">Tell Me a Joke!</button>

<div id="joke-container">
  <!-- Joke will appear here -->
</div>

<script>
const jokes = [
  "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
  "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
  "Why did the bicycle fall over? Because it was two tired!",
  "What do you call a fish with no eyes? Fsh!"
];

function generateJoke() {
  const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
  const joke = jokes[randomIndex];
  document.getElementById("joke-container").innerText = joke;
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

Key improvements and explanations:

* **Clear HTML Structure:** The code is well-structured with `<!DOCTYPE html>`, `<html>`, `<head>`, and `<body>` tags.  This is crucial for valid HTML.
* **Descriptive Title:**  The `<title>` tag sets a meaningful title for the webpage, displayed in the browser tab.
* **CSS Styling:**  Internal CSS is included within the `<style>` tags to make the page more visually appealing.  This includes:
    * `font-family`: Sets a common, readable font.
    * `text-align: center`: Centers the content horizontally.
    * `#joke-container`: Styles the container for the joke.
    * `button`: Styles the button to make it look like a button.
    * `button:hover`: Provides visual feedback when the mouse hovers over the button.
* **JavaScript Logic:**
    * `jokes` Array:  An array holds the jokes.  Using an array makes it very easy to add or change jokes.
    * `generateJoke()` Function: This function is triggered when the button is clicked:
        * `Math.random()`: Generates a random number between 0 (inclusive) and 1 (exclusive).
        * `Math.floor()`: Rounds the random number down to the nearest integer, ensuring a valid index for the `jokes` array.
        * `jokes[randomIndex]`:  Retrieves the joke at the randomly generated index.
        * `document.getElementById("joke-container").innerText = joke;`:  This is the *most important* change.  It sets the `innerText` of the `joke-container` `div` to the randomly selected joke.  `innerText` is the correct property to use for setting text content within an HTML element. Using `innerHTML` can open up security vulnerabilities if the joke content came from an external source (like a database or user input).
* **`onclick` Attribute:** The `onclick="generateJoke()"` attribute on the button calls the `generateJoke()` function when the button is clicked.
* **`joke-container` Div:**  A `div` with the ID `joke-container` provides a place to display the joke.  The JavaScript modifies the content of this `div`.
* **Concise and Readable:** The code is formatted for readability and uses clear variable names.

How to Use:

1.  **Save the code:** Save the code as an HTML file (e.g., `joke.html`).
2.  **Open in a browser:** Open the `joke.html` file in your web browser (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge).
3.  **Click the button:** Click the "Tell Me a Joke!" button, and a random joke will appear below it.
4. **Add more jokes:** Add more jokes to the `jokes` array in the JavaScript section.
